(
 'How to Build a Cloud Portfolio That Gets You Interviews',
 'cloud-portfolio-projects-that-get-interviews',
 'Certifications get you screened; projects get you hired. Four portfolio projects, how to document them, and the mistakes that make recruiters skip your GitHub.',
 E'Most junior cloud CVs look identical: a certification, a tutorial to-do app, and a list of service names. The candidates who get called back have two or three projects that look like work someone would actually pay for.\n\n## What hiring managers look for\n\n1. **It is deployed and reachable**, not just a repository of YAML.\n2. **It is reproducible** — infrastructure as code, not click-ops you cannot repeat.\n3. **You can explain the trade-offs** — why this database, why this network layout, what it costs per month.\n4. **It fails safely** — health checks, backups, alerts.\n\n## Four projects worth building\n\n### 1. A production-shaped web application\n\nA frontend, an API and a managed database, deployed with Terraform, behind HTTPS with a real domain. Add a staging environment using the same code with different variables.\n\n*Shows:* networking, IaC, TLS, environment separation.\n\n### 2. A CI/CD pipeline you did not copy\n\nBuild, test, scan, push an image, deploy on merge, with a manual approval gate for production and an automated rollback path.\n\n*Shows:* automation, release safety, security awareness.\n\n### 3. An observability and incident write-up\n\nAdd metrics, logs and dashboards to project 1. Then break it deliberately, detect it through your alerting, fix it, and publish a short post-incident review.\n\n*Shows:* operational maturity — rare in juniors and heavily weighted in interviews.\n\n### 4. A cost optimisation case study\n\nRun something for a month, capture the bill, then right-size it, add autoscaling or lifecycle rules, and document the before-and-after with your own numbers.\n\n*Shows:* commercial judgement, which is what separates an engineer from a lab hobbyist.\n\n## How to document each project\n\nA README with: the problem in two sentences, an architecture diagram, how to deploy it in one command, the decisions you made and rejected, the monthly running cost, and what you would do next with more time. Add a 90-second screen recording — many reviewers will not clone your repository.\n\n## Mistakes that get you skipped\n\n- Secrets committed to the repository. This ends the review immediately.\n- Fifteen half-finished repositories. Pin three and archive the rest.\n- No diagram. If a reviewer cannot understand your system in 30 seconds, they move on.\n- Copied tutorial names in your commit history with no changes of your own.\n\n## Turning the portfolio into interviews\n\nWrite one short post per project and link it from your CV and LinkedIn. Mention the specific technologies in the first line so recruiters searching those terms find you. Then apply to roles that name those same tools.\n\nIf you want your projects reviewed by working engineers before you publish them, our [live cohort programmes](/courses) include code and architecture review, graded assignments and mock interviews, and graduates can apply through our [job board](/jobs). New to cloud entirely? Start with [how to become a cloud engineer in 2026](/blog/how-to-become-a-cloud-engineer-in-2026).\n\n## Frequently asked\n\n**How many projects do I need?** Three well-documented projects beat ten unfinished ones.\n\n**Do projects matter more than certifications?** Certifications pass the screening filter; projects win the technical interview. You want both, in that order of effort.\n\n**What if I cannot afford cloud costs?** Use free tiers, destroy resources after each session with `terraform destroy`, and set a hard budget alert.',
 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1461749280684-dccba630e2f6?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80',
 'Silicon Edge Consulting', 'published', 'Careers',
 ARRAY['portfolio','career','cloud','devops','interviews'],
